    "keyTakeaways": [
      "DNS is the automated phonebook that turns names into IP addresses before almost every connection; a client asks one recursive question, and the resolver performs several iterative hops (root -> TLD -> authoritative) behind the scenes to build the final answer.",
      "Because a name lookup precedes almost every connection, DNS logs at the recursive resolver capture each host's intent early and centrally — making them the first pivot for spotting malicious destinations, newly registered domains, and DGA activity.",
      "Record types reveal intent: A/AAAA show what's being reached, TXT is a favorite channel for tunneling and SPF text, MX shows mail intent, and PTR reverses an IP back to a name — recognizing the type in a log line tells you what a host was trying to do.",
      "DNSSEC protects answer integrity but not privacy, while DoH/DoT encrypt lookups and can blind network DNS monitoring — so force clients to the internal logged resolver, detect external DoH, and rely on endpoint DNS telemetry where needed.",
      "DGA traffic looks like many different high-entropy domains mostly failing with NXDOMAIN in a short burst; DNS tunneling looks like endless unique subdomains (often base64-like, often TXT records) hammering the same parent domain — learning to tell the two shapes apart is a core DNS-hunting skill."
    ],
