      "Long-lived access keys are the credential that leaks — into Git, CI logs, laptops, and images — and because the key itself is the proof of identity, a thief needs no password and faces no MFA, so prefer roles/temporary credentials, rotate, scope tightly, and never commit keys.",
      "Permission evaluation is default-deny with an explicit allow required and an explicit deny always winning; a leaked credential's actual damage is bounded by its attached policy, so least-privilege scoping (specific actions, specific resources, conditions like MFA) is what separates a contained incident from a full takeover — as the leaked-key case study shows step by step.",
